{
  "description": "What a call held for approval keeps beside its parsed form, and what the approved call runs with (#275), written by hand. approval.args is the parsed form, which holds numbers as float64s and reads a lone surrogate as a replacement character, so args cannot say every text: the person is shown the text and the approved call runs it. items are approvals as a Go writer keeps them, always in a checkpoint of version 5 or 6. keeps: whether a Go writer keeps a call's text, which it does wherever args cannot say it. It keeps none where args cannot have changed it, so an ordinary call's record is what a published release wrote, and whitespace, member order and a string's escapes are not kept: args are the canonical form at every depth, which approval_requested shows and the approved call runs. Two names that fold together are refused before anyone is asked, so they are not here. approved: what an approved record runs with: go is the bytes that run or refused, and typescript the value that runs or refused. A record with no request is what a published release wrote, so Go runs its args only where they cannot have changed the call, and otherwise refuses it as stale. TypeScript runs what JSON.parse read, so it takes a kept text only where that reading says it exactly and reads back to args, and its writer refuses what JSON.parse rounded after that. Go's parsed form cannot show every change: 7.0000000000000000001 reads as 7. go/context/durable_writes_test.go and toolSources.test.ts read these, and tests/test_contract.py derives each from its text. shown: the text a host renders for a call and shows as a string, never through a parsed value, and the digest an approval reference binds to: the SHA-256 of that text's UTF-8 bytes, approvalShownArguments and approvalShownDigest in toolSources.ts, ApprovalShownArguments and ApprovalShownDigest in go/context/sources.go. A digest over args cannot tell 9007199254740992 from 9007199254740993. bindings: the digest of the call the package's own approval wait binds to, which covers the kept text, so another spelling of one parsed form is another call. decisions: what a decision must prove. Approving a call whose record keeps its text needs shownDigest, and a digest sent must match, so a host that has not moved to the readers refuses by name rather than approving one number while another runs.",
